#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082989 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "5c0c0cf3-3d1c-5d6b-8835-d73c97e4cb39" :question/math-academy-id "e-6722" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=2x^3-3x^2+5.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+5) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ 6x(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x=0,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=0$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082990 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "882080a6-fbb0-512f-ab9f-5a8ad0686317" :question/math-academy-id "e-6723" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x) = e^{x^3-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-1){e}^{{x}^{3}-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-1){e}^{{x}^{3}-x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{x^3-x} \\neq 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by $e^{x^3-x}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-1){e}^{{x}^{3}-x} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083044 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "a6af5246-ef2e-5b52-8ec7-2bc861b1398d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8757" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac {x-3}{x^2 -6}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-6)-(x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-6)-(x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6+6x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+6}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+6}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =3 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=3\\pm \\sqrt 3.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt 6.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=3\\pm \\sqrt 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083051 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Critical Points of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "d8a14698-9de0-53ec-96b3-2ee20f14d5e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9576" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = \\sqrt 3\\sin{2x} - \\cos{2x}.$ Find critical points of $f(x)$ for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c) = 0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡2x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ are \n\n$$\nx =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$ are\n\n$$\nx_1 =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x_2 =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Critical Points of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a716a49-51ec-54e0-ae4e-94352d1c8d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280666} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d7a4ed-32b8-54d7-9197-4f0546245ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280667} {:answer/id #uuid "1600cac6-2d8b-56bd-afe7-4f137ba3fcf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280668} {:answer/id #uuid "109c70b9-d380-527e-8bce-a2dfaa037768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280669} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ee4789-2daf-59bf-bab5-a5064d6cc2a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6d7a4ed-32b8-54d7-9197-4f0546245ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280667} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dea5912-59da-54f5-a993-0b791e756393" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3453a5c2-1ed2-5148-a17a-c5359d25adf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127391" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+n\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (0,2\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14de8f15-42e5-5e5a-a8ba-790e42857927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280673} {:answer/id #uuid "69e43028-d7a3-576b-8b1b-96256c8d3cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280674} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec5b48f-0971-518e-82b4-b55e96db9440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=9$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280675} {:answer/id #uuid "a60f2d6f-75f8-5f68-b8e5-a56ab18e90dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280676} {:answer/id #uuid "5b305ee6-8380-5997-98ae-86cdd4dbca88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-9$ and $x=9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14de8f15-42e5-5e5a-a8ba-790e42857927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280673} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c31a122-f7a5-5bd4-91ae-ef3037fd761a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78cd88cf-0938-5eca-ad33-9a676a3cd4cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-131185" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}+6x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x} \\\\  & =(2x+6)\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+6)\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+6)\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+6 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-3.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $f(x)$ is $x=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54fbfe87-1aae-5994-a8a6-dcb155d90045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280680} {:answer/id #uuid "8b86c963-27af-515f-a734-1123ac78b729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280681} {:answer/id #uuid "df14003c-31e1-58e7-9ad1-d01204e7091e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280682} {:answer/id #uuid "c9824cdf-3af6-564a-9129-f1a551721ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280683} {:answer/id #uuid "285a0db0-558f-57ba-924c-e6d9319d269e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b86c963-27af-515f-a734-1123ac78b729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280681} :answer-field/id #uuid "71c29374-a391-5932-a35e-e000c60c287e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19a51c3a-961d-51e8-839d-dcbc2e643a8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-131188" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-72x.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-72x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x-72 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcf2f269-1631-5ce9-b6d1-5f62a9e1cd57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280687} {:answer/id #uuid "e914e1f3-57bd-5e74-a152-921dea275d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\pm 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280688} {:answer/id #uuid "5aeee68d-01ca-5caa-8229-babe4a18c414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280689} {:answer/id #uuid "55d176e1-643e-54db-96c0-f0a1d17deb29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280690} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6648cf-52ab-5eff-aa0f-8735884d2f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e914e1f3-57bd-5e74-a152-921dea275d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\pm 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280688} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eaa94aa-f031-5e66-9fc2-68eee4c02c08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42598c74-a5de-588f-a397-c6d324d9491a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131273" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)=({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4})}^{′}{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){({e}^{4{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+(8x)({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =[2x+8{x}^{3}-10x]{e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-8x & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 8x(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,-1,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Critical Points of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c96e002-b3b3-52c5-ade0-ddfb4c83c758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280694} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3db0aa-2398-5051-9911-e6c3902fb470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280695} {:answer/id #uuid "80659f67-523a-5486-afad-81d6d4569db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280696} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfcefc6-fe91-5588-84ab-9b06f0fcf4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280697} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1f65e1-7ecb-578c-a13b-dd59d0aa9fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bfcefc6-fe91-5588-84ab-9b06f0fcf4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280697} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc73a482-06bc-563c-93cd-bc92a151934c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf3436ad-e1fe-557d-8a36-acddfc3947bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132567" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Critical Points of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f61b090-6084-58c4-bba2-535c643863ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280701} {:answer/id #uuid "0c728fdc-e6f1-5b2d-ba09-1afdb5e76239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280702} {:answer/id #uuid "3d92fb77-03c9-59a6-9b42-eda731a3fc62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280703} {:answer/id #uuid "fa97072a-0f11-550a-aed1-36df11609980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280704} {:answer/id #uuid "c835c3a6-4a11-50dc-a36f-f32e59d3c9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa97072a-0f11-550a-aed1-36df11609980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280704} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cd341b6-4e5e-5216-87f1-95aa03fd0805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7337738-5ff0-5479-90bf-c4348b276c02" :question/math-academy-id "q-132569" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{7}{2})+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈1.292\\,50$ rounded to five decimal places\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x≈-1.849\\,10$ rounded to five decimal places\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx≈1.292\\,50,\\,x≈-1.849\\,10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-1.849\\,10$ and $x≈1.292\\,50.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}≈-1.849\\,10$ and ${x}_{2}≈1.292\\,50.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-1.849\\,10\\cdot 1.292\\,50≈-2.39\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2c78ab6-b2a5-5fc9-8102-df0059fd902e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ and $x = 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080638} {:answer/id #uuid "ef316532-efa8-50fc-bc38-4613ba578a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080639} {:answer/id #uuid "194303dc-be8d-5457-856f-18cf5cfcdb23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080640} {:answer/id #uuid "4463d2ee-c670-5ed4-81e0-ad6ef7f0c4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-4$ and $x =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080641} {:answer/id #uuid "66969161-9cda-5d4a-bab4-3f6de8206bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-4$ and $x = 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef316532-efa8-50fc-bc38-4613ba578a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080639} :answer-field/id #uuid "831eff03-6277-5b7f-9b25-50ae2e5b06e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00fe70db-ae72-5c44-af16-89ddf096e915" :question/math-academy-id "q-21495" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+24x+9.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+24x+9) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-30x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-30x+24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Critical Points of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66e29b20-5353-5798-9c3d-e33a9b89e0e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66e29b20-5353-5798-9c3d-e33a9b89e0e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280708} :answer-field/id #uuid "36769b3f-f06c-5e2c-9870-7522211fbcb7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280707} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eb4e0da-df1c-51b1-8a75-f3c9a7ea8475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2eb4e0da-df1c-51b1-8a75-f3c9a7ea8475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280710} :answer-field/id #uuid "a87998e2-01a6-5635-811c-9e1845a1e9b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b86cc57-835e-5c4e-92f9-4753683d5914" :question/math-academy-id "q-314779" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$\n\n- setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (0,2\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "919dc54f-5cc5-52ba-abe2-ef8a234b0ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "919dc54f-5cc5-52ba-abe2-ef8a234b0ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280713} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a6531eb-b67a-5542-9e05-a69455577972" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280712} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346db744-fd1a-56a4-9fb3-c6a9538fd256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "346db744-fd1a-56a4-9fb3-c6a9538fd256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280715} :answer-field/id #uuid "61091b4e-5f84-58d2-b183-e89cbfab11ba" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f148c92-f658-549e-a0cb-5ac12616def8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344544" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+24x-11$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+24x-11) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+18x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+18x+24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=-2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-4$ and $x=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12770882-c5bc-5e68-9a98-68df38b775ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280718} {:answer/id #uuid "05240ba4-db34-576a-90ed-c51e45ea9162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05240ba4-db34-576a-90ed-c51e45ea9162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280719} :answer-field/id #uuid "932d3d2a-75ab-5536-850a-f820d7653d7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280717} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c619e9-cd92-5e27-aad9-c6625c36ee9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30c619e9-cd92-5e27-aad9-c6625c36ee9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280721} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5c5fc8-79b7-5493-a1d2-f5bab2ec1c57" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d80a50a6-7416-5124-9e38-1f3238eca785" :question/math-academy-id "q-344841" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2})}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-12x){e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-12x){e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are  $x=0$ and  $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6365089c-ca2d-5190-84f6-7bb5774db6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6365089c-ca2d-5190-84f6-7bb5774db6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280724} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bc40150-6359-5379-bf14-bc3763ee9fd9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280723} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f8aad47-7111-501c-a0e7-3e02ef73e2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f8aad47-7111-501c-a0e7-3e02ef73e2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280726} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd796db4-1258-57be-90fe-ae8a87711227" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ed5c2a5-9f71-5933-881b-13c938f0f660" :question/math-academy-id "q-344857" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)=(x+1)^{3}{e}^{x-1}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+1)^{3}{e}^{x-1}) \\\\  & ={((x+1)^{3})}^{′}{e}^{x-1}+(x+1)^{3}{({e}^{x-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =3(x+1)^{2}{e}^{x-1}+(x+1)^{3}{e}^{x-1} \\\\  & =[3(x+1)^{2}+(x+1)^{3}]{e}^{x-1} \\\\  & =(x+1)^{2}(x+4){e}^{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}(x+4){e}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x-1}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{x-1}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=-1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9e3ec4d-9089-5218-903e-ead0e59b1084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9e3ec4d-9089-5218-903e-ead0e59b1084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280729} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fee5fab-e353-5007-85ff-a4867975b0fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280728} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f0ef56e-679b-525c-a933-85b1ae11aeb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f0ef56e-679b-525c-a933-85b1ae11aeb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280731} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbcc8226-2ba9-5f7f-b76c-8c32d3f53b0e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280730} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cca3c2e-81ae-516c-8a16-a19e2a8671fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cca3c2e-81ae-516c-8a16-a19e2a8671fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280733} :answer-field/id #uuid "642d4508-277d-5b8c-ab1f-a06ed3fc6689" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e050c4e-a3a3-5fee-80dd-c16191e31da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344861" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function  $f(x)=({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-1)}^{′}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-1){({e}^{-{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-1)(-2x){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}(1-({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2x(2-{x}^{2}){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2x({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x({x}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ -2x(x-\\sqrt{2})(x+\\sqrt{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\sqrt{2},\\,0,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ $x=0,$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=0,\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1b5578c-54d6-518a-a821-205041416eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1b5578c-54d6-518a-a821-205041416eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280736} :answer-field/id #uuid "47cfa3b4-4572-5b09-ba58-7f4870cf5b27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280735} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01269afc-9ab8-5379-b77a-d6e39aa9dfa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01269afc-9ab8-5379-b77a-d6e39aa9dfa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280738} :answer-field/id #uuid "70591605-abc1-5f53-968f-adbeabf56a79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bba6115-49a3-547a-a267-5ba20a9dc0de" :question/math-academy-id "q-344867" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function  $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+8}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)-(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)-(x+1)\\cdot (2x)}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+8-(2{x}^{2}+2x)}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}-2x+8}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-2x+8}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-2x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So, $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are \n\n$$\nx=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,x=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Critical Points of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81fe41b7-4979-5a64-8a1b-70d5ba6404ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81fe41b7-4979-5a64-8a1b-70d5ba6404ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280741} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dbd8c01-b6de-5716-bc3b-e918932d845a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280740} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ef4e134-4fb0-5489-b214-2b4a9f035fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ef4e134-4fb0-5489-b214-2b4a9f035fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280743} :answer-field/id #uuid "c27c3136-3431-56ab-9dfb-5cb88c60e1c8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffec8fbc-60c6-5424-a03e-5c676c82209f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345502" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6b2763f-1dc4-52a2-bd45-5220372c5263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081213} {:answer/id #uuid "ff94c620-29e9-5e70-9d2c-14ee19fa4ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081214} {:answer/id #uuid "040b9fdd-9a59-5fe4-802f-dd97d37afddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081215} {:answer/id #uuid "44fadb91-50e5-5837-9b9f-0cd247d561b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-\\sqrt{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081216} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2bef42-0410-5bca-9b21-e54a0af5f868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d2bef42-0410-5bca-9b21-e54a0af5f868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081217} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbfb4a98-5054-58d1-88f3-28535864c817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5947b13d-c33a-5e29-b427-e8d6513a809d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48741" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+4)-x\\cdot ({x}^{2}+4)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd4b81be-cb1e-5fa2-97c7-f8cc896652aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280746} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f8178d-1c28-5856-9c84-fde98681ad8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280747} {:answer/id #uuid "9f601d0e-caf2-5198-962b-b5a5172bffbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280748} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb88612-80fa-55c1-93f4-ca16c52bbb56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280749} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed924b9-09ab-549d-ba69-254be037cb45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2f8178d-1c28-5856-9c84-fde98681ad8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280747} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bd752b4-ddd6-5a48-8a52-6e03dd3f1a55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "811d06c8-d944-5b16-a846-13c01b72d3b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48922" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+3)-(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+3)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+3)-(x+1)\\cdot (2x)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3-2{x}^{2}-2x}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-2x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3-2x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 3-2x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fd83c29-8419-54c7-abba-d5f069a83ada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280753} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e9f02b-82d3-5fe4-a735-dd0b9ce57ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280754} {:answer/id #uuid "9b042981-4567-54bb-97a1-e0bef40c8eb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280755} {:answer/id #uuid "bffc64cd-0a1d-5c8f-bc82-2285c43e402a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{4}{3}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280756} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0a2bdb-c2ec-5b2c-a120-564ba7129bcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b042981-4567-54bb-97a1-e0bef40c8eb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280755} :answer-field/id #uuid "df39f18b-7277-5e61-bf4a-6474dbd3c6e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cce01680-c8ac-5738-b576-171ef08963ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-99019" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+12x+13.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+12x+13) \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}+24x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+24x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+8x+4 & =0 \\\\ (3x+2)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e87c79c-fea0-559e-934f-f6ccc2921839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280760} {:answer/id #uuid "144e1c59-0d9c-5a61-9aeb-aaaf350922e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280761} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c58a7c-9d19-5b06-8aa7-58480d5eae11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280762} {:answer/id #uuid "f36736f3-8c5e-5f4d-bf2b-ff007bc07c98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=-6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280763} {:answer/id #uuid "05923af4-d390-526c-8df8-e27fd8bb88c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7c58a7c-9d19-5b06-8aa7-58480d5eae11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280762} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ccf8016-a8fc-5c9d-8364-dcdd8611c233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a08e4af-7f63-5439-b490-91f36e22b19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99032" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+21x+23.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+21x+23) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+24x+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+24x+21 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+7 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+7) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\,-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-1$ and $x=-7.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05781d01-edac-50e4-8c7e-f6d1a7352445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280767} {:answer/id #uuid "478d8141-35d1-5fcf-89e5-75f83f0e847b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280768} {:answer/id #uuid "7ced6d18-640e-5682-af98-fb3d7c2a0eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280769} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3755f9-801a-5a08-af24-f58c4cd9755e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280770} {:answer/id #uuid "a33f0e13-f1b8-58f5-af37-6f5d4fcddf2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "478d8141-35d1-5fcf-89e5-75f83f0e847b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280768} :answer-field/id #uuid "3480d180-4a78-572e-b2ac-cf2de69f3f0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42dd53a1-4ccb-5086-adb5-70b77ded4e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-99053" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+6}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(2x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(2x+1)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+12-4{x}^{2}-2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-2x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12-2x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 12-2x-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27548e7f-003a-5c17-957d-ead77ed104e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280774} {:answer/id #uuid "ed53dbec-72a9-52d1-aafe-4adde0b83d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280775} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ec8f46-3f19-5428-bd72-e1b30b2308ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280776} {:answer/id #uuid "2f38057c-3792-5bee-b2c7-3e25851fa860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280777} {:answer/id #uuid "57986211-8a2c-5c12-929b-3f3350de3c24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed53dbec-72a9-52d1-aafe-4adde0b83d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280775} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f42e289-fda4-50ef-86d2-078bbf460904" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "70a98919-dd4e-535e-b918-4ce91d1560b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-99056" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+6x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$"}]}
